---
/**
 * Landing FAQ.
 *
 * These are the objections the product actually raises and the site answered
 * nowhere: whether it is another Electron editor fork, whether it sits between
 * an agent and its provider, what happens to a session on restart, what it
 * costs. Every answer here has to stay true to the app — if behaviour changes,
 * this file changes with it.
 *
 * Rendered as <details> so it works with JS off and costs nothing.
 */
const FAQS = [
  {
    q: 'Is this a VS Code or Electron fork?',
    a: 'No. maiTerm is a Tauri 2 desktop app using the operating system\'s own webview — WebKit on macOS, WebView2 on Windows, WebKitGTK on Linux. There is no bundled browser and no editor fork. The terminal itself is Rust: alacritty_terminal does the VTE parsing and buffering in the backend.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does maiTerm see my API keys or proxy my prompts?',
    a: 'No. maiTerm launches the agent CLIs already installed on your machine and they keep using their own accounts, authentication and configuration. Your conversations go from that local CLI to its provider. maiTerm is not a model gateway and never asks you for a provider key. The one thing it can sit in the path of is an MCP server you choose to add in Preferences → MCP servers: then maiTerm holds that server\'s sign-in and passes your agents\' tool calls to it, without writing their arguments or results to its log.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can maiTerm manage my MCP servers?',
    a: 'The remote ones, if you want it to. Add a server in Preferences → MCP servers and sign in once: maiTerm keeps the sign-in in your system\'s credential store, refreshes it, and serves the server to Claude Code under every account, to Codex, and to Claude Code on your SSH hosts — where, on a machine several people share, each person\'s agents use their own sign-in. A server that goes down keeps its tools instead of vanishing mid-task. It is opt-in per server, and servers you installed directly keep working untouched. Local servers started with a command, Codex over SSH and Gemini aren\'t covered yet.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can my agent see what is going wrong in my browser?',
    a: 'Yes, in one tab you hand it. With the maiTerm extension for Chrome (and other Chromium browsers), you link a tab to an agent from the extension\'s icon, and that agent can read the tab\'s console errors, its failing requests and the responses they got back, and the page itself, and can click, type, navigate and take screenshots in it — in your own signed-in session, in the background. No agent can link a tab or see your others, and each agent works only the tab linked to it. The extension talks only to maiTerm on your own computer. Turn it on in Preferences → Chrome extension; until its Chrome Web Store listing is live, maiTerm walks you through loading it unpacked.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I be signed in to two Claude accounts at once?',
    a: 'Yes, and Claude Code on its own cannot — it has a single login slot, shared by every terminal you open. maiTerm gives each account its own configuration directory and starts each new tab under whichever account you have made active, so a client\'s account can be working in one tab while your own runs in the next. For those local tabs it never holds the credential: the agent does its own sign-in, refresh and sign-out, and maiTerm owns only the folder. SSH tabs can run as the active account too, on hosts you choose — that needs a long-lived token, which maiTerm keeps in your system keychain, so it is opt-in per account. Off by default, macOS and Linux for now.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which coding agents does it support?',
    a: 'Claude Code and OpenAI Codex, through one runtime-neutral pipeline — you pick which ones maiTerm wires up in Preferences. Any other CLI still runs in a tab like it would in any terminal; it just does not get the agent-aware features.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there an LLM built into the terminal?',
    a: 'No, and that is deliberate. There is no chat sidebar and no model maiTerm calls on your behalf. It manages the agents you run, rather than being another one.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens when I restart, or it crashes?',
    a: 'Every tab comes back as it was: scrollback, pane layout, working directories, SSH sessions and agent sessions. Each resumed agent reconnects to its tab on its own, with nothing typed by anyone. Scrollback persists to SQLite in WAL mode, so an unclean exit does not take the buffer with it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it work over SSH?',
    a: 'Yes, and it is a first-class path rather than an afterthought. Agents on a remote host get the same tab identity, task board, notes and MCP tools through a reverse tunnel. Remote files open in the editor transparently, and file paths in output are clickable the same way.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do my agents get to change things without me?',
    a: 'Only what you allow. Agents may move tabs and workspaces around, but anything that changes which agents can message each other, opens a new agent tab, reloads another agent\'s tab or a window, or restarts maiTerm is only a request: a card in the Loom and on your paired phone, worded by maiTerm, that nothing but your click approves — not an agent asking you in chat, and not a permission dialog. Overlord ships in propose mode: every directive is held for your approval showing the exact words it would send, and your own keystrokes abort a sequence in progress. The rule guards are human-only and cannot be edited by an agent. On the task board an agent retracts misread work to a lane you can reverse, and the rows it may delete outright never include another tab\'s work in progress or a task something unfinished still waits on. A script an agent asks maiTerm to run for it later runs only once you have approved that exact script. And nothing maiTerm sends for you — a message from your phone or a peer agent, a chat pickup — is submitted along with something you have half-typed into a Claude Code agent\'s input box: in a tab running maiTerm\'s Claude Code mod it goes in beside your draft, which stays put, and otherwise it waits until you have sent or cleared it. Nor, on Claude Code versions that support mods, can an agent in a maiTerm tab message other Claude sessions on the machine behind your back: the mesh and bridges you set up are its only way across.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens if I rename or move a project folder?',
    a: 'Use Move project… on the workspace and nothing is left behind. maiTerm pauses the agents working in the folder, moves it, and points everything at the new location — tabs, services, follow-ups, and each agent\'s own record of the project: Claude Code\'s sessions, memory and folder trust, Codex\'s trust and threads, Gemini\'s registry — then resumes each agent in the same session. Moved it outside maiTerm? A tab whose folder has gone opens in your home folder without resuming its agent there, and asks you where the folder went.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Claude Code can already schedule things. Why follow-ups?',
    a: 'Its own schedules either live inside the running Claude process or start a fresh session in the cloud, on a clone of your repo. A maiTerm follow-up is held by maiTerm and delivered back into the same tab — the same session, your local services and files, your SSH hosts — and it survives the agent exiting and maiTerm restarting. It can wait on your workspace, not only the clock: a service coming up, a task finishing, or a check the agent wrote. It works for Codex too, and you can see and cancel every one that is pending.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does it cost?',
    a: 'Nothing. Every feature is included — there is no paid tier, no licence check and no account.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What licence is it under?',
    a: 'Source available, not OSI open source. The full source is published and you may use, modify, distribute and sublicense it; the one restriction is that you may not use it to build a competing terminal emulator. Building it into something with a substantially different purpose — an IDE, a DevOps platform — is explicitly allowed. The terms are in LICENSE in the repository.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which platforms does it run on?',
    a: 'macOS (Apple Silicon only — there is no Intel build), Windows 10 and later as a 64-bit installer, and Linux as an AppImage or .deb built against webkit2gtk-4.1 on Ubuntu 24.04.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the Claude Code integration work on Windows?',
    a: 'Yes, with Git for Windows installed: Claude Code runs its commands through Git Bash, and so do the hooks that tell maiTerm which tab a session is in. A setup wizard checks for Git, Claude Code and whether claude is on your PATH, and installs or fixes what is missing in visible tabs. New tabs use Git Bash when it is installed. Windows support for Claude is new and less used than macOS, and the Claude Code mod is not loaded there yet.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it phone home?',
    a: 'Only the update check, and it is counted. That check reaches a small Cloudflare Worker which records one row per unique user per day — a salted hash of IP and user-agent, where the salt is random, rotates daily and is then discarded, so it cannot be reversed or linked across days. No IP, user-agent, cookie or device ID is stored, and nothing about your projects, tabs, terminal output or agents ever leaves the machine. There is no other analytics, no error uploads and no account. maiTerm also ships a Preferences section that switches off the telemetry and error reporting in the agent CLIs it launches, and it turns Claude Code’s Remote Control off by default, so your sessions are not exposed to claude.ai or the Claude mobile app unless you switch it back on.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do updates work?',
    a: 'maiTerm checks GitHub Releases automatically and installs an update with one click, showing you the release notes first. You can turn the startup check off in Preferences and update from the menu instead.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I get my state onto another machine?',
    a: 'Yes. Export workspaces, tabs, scrollback, notes, triggers and preferences to a single archive — on a schedule if you want, with compression and auto-trim. Importing previews what is inside so you can pick which workspaces to bring in and whether to merge or overwrite.',
  },
];

// Split into two columns without reflowing the reading order down one side.
const half = Math.ceil(FAQS.length / 2);
const columns = [FAQS.slice(0, half), FAQS.slice(half)];
---

<section class="ln-band" id="faq">
  <div class="ln-inner">
    <p class="ln-eyebrow">Questions</p>
    <h2 class="ln-h2 ln-h2--wide">Frequently asked questions</h2>

    <div class="faq-cols">
      {
        columns.map((col) => (
          <div class="faq-col">
            {col.map((f) => (
              <details class="faq-item">
                <summary>
                  <span>{f.q}</span>
                  <svg
                    class="faq-chevron"
                    width="16"
                    height="16"
                    viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                    fill="none"
                    stroke="currentColor"
                    stroke-width="2.5"
                    stroke-linecap="round"
                    stroke-linejoin="round"
                    aria-hidden="true"
                  >
                    <path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6" />
                  </svg>
                </summary>
                <p>{f.a}</p>
              </details>
            ))}
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .faq-cols {
    margin-top: 2.75rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0 3rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  @media (min-width: 56rem) {
    .faq-cols {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }
  }

  .faq-col {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  .faq-item {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ln-border);
  }

  .faq-item summary {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    padding: 1.15rem 0;
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
    /* The docks taught this one: real question text overflows a half-width
       column, so let it wrap rather than clip. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .faq-item summary::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }
  .faq-item summary:hover {
    color: var(--ln-accent-soft);
  }

  .faq-chevron {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    color: var(--ln-fg-faint);
    transition: transform 0.18s ease;
  }
  .faq-item[open] .faq-chevron {
    transform: rotate(180deg);
  }

  .faq-item p {
    padding-bottom: 1.25rem;
    margin-top: -0.25rem;
    color: var(--ln-fg-dim);
    font-size: 0.975rem;
    line-height: 1.62;
    max-width: 60ch;
  }
</style>
